IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), Part III: Non-Contributory Benefits,
-- ss 63-67 (descriptions of non-contributory benefits; attendance allowance). LAYER 1: the law, objectively.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering by a converter script, not the official text (see NOTES.md).
-- Reading a result: JUST TRUE / JUST FALSE / NOTHING. JUST FALSE only where the text says no in its own words;
-- NOTHING is "not covered" (silent, list not closed, includes, undefined term, points outside the text,
-- limited by its own wording). A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- A BAR function (a "shall not be entitled" rule) answers: JUST FALSE = the text says not entitled;
-- NOTHING = the rule is silent on the case; never JUST TRUE, because a bar grants nothing.

§ `Part III -- Non-Contributory Benefits`

§§ `s 63 -- Descriptions of non-contributory benefits`

-- s 63: the list "of the following descriptions, namely" is not closed by "only", so a benefit it does not name is NOTHING.
-- src:1913 | 63 Descriptions of non-contributory benefits.
-- src:1914 | Non-contributory benefits under this Part of this Act are of the following descriptions, namely—
-- src:1915 | (a) attendance allowance;
-- src:1916 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:1917 | (c) carer’s allowance...;
-- src:1918 | (d) disability living allowance;
-- src:1919 | (e) guardian’s allowance;
-- src:1920 | (f) retirement pensions of the following categories—
-- src:1921 | (i) Category C, payable in certain cases to a widow whose husband was over pensionable age on 5 July 1948 or to a woman whose marriage to a husband who was over pensionable age on that date was terminated otherwise than by his death (with increase for adult ... dependants), and
-- src:1922 | (ii) Category D, payable to persons over the age of 80;
-- src:1923 | (g) age addition payable, in the case of persons over the age of 80, by way of increase of a retirement pension of any category under this Act or of some other pension or allowance from the Secretary of State.
@ref s 63 (src:1913-1923)
GIVEN benefit IS A Benefit
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63 the benefit is a non-contributory benefit under Part III` MEANS
    CONSIDER benefit
    WHEN `attendance allowance`          THEN JUST TRUE    -- (a)
    WHEN `carer's allowance`             THEN JUST TRUE    -- (c)
    WHEN `disability living allowance`   THEN JUST TRUE    -- (d)
    WHEN `guardian's allowance`          THEN JUST TRUE    -- (e)
    WHEN `Category C retirement pension` THEN JUST TRUE    -- (f)(i)
    WHEN `Category D retirement pension` THEN JUST TRUE    -- (f)(ii)
    WHEN `age addition`                  THEN JUST TRUE    -- (g)
    OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "are of the following descriptions, namely" has no "only"; severe disablement allowance, para (b), is blank in this text

-- s 63(f)(i): a description of who a Category C pension is payable to. "payable in certain cases to" does not close the class.
-- src:1921 | (i) Category C, payable in certain cases to a widow whose husband was over pensionable age on 5 July 1948 or to a woman whose marriage to a husband who was over pensionable age on that date was terminated otherwise than by his death (with increase for adult ... dependants), and
@ref s 63(f)(i) (src:1921)
GIVEN `she is a widow` IS A BOOLEAN
      `her marriage was terminated otherwise than by her husband's death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `her husband was over pensionable age on 5 July 1948` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(f)(i) a Category C retirement pension is payable in the case described` MEANS
    IF   `her husband was over pensionable age on 5 July 1948`
         AND (`she is a widow` OR `her marriage was terminated otherwise than by her husband's death`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "payable in certain cases to"; the other cases are not described here

-- s 63(f)(ii): Category D "payable to persons over the age of 80". K2: s 173(a) says over 80 means has attained 80, and the answer is an input.
-- src:1922 | (ii) Category D, payable to persons over the age of 80;
@ref s 63(f)(ii) (src:1922)
GIVEN `s 173(a) the person is over 80` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(f)(ii) a Category D retirement pension is payable to the person` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 173(a) the person is over 80`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "payable to persons over the age of 80" describes who gets it and does not say who does not; s 78(3) holds the conditions

-- s 63(g): age addition "payable, in the case of persons over the age of 80, by way of increase of a retirement pension ... or of some other pension or allowance from the Secretary of State".
-- src:1923 | (g) age addition payable, in the case of persons over the age of 80, by way of increase of a retirement pension of any category under this Act or of some other pension or allowance from the Secretary of State.
@ref s 63(g) (src:1923)
GIVEN `s 173(a) the person is over 80` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `it is an increase of a retirement pension of a category under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an increase of some other pension or allowance from the Secretary of State` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(g) an age addition is payable in the case described` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 173(a) the person is over 80`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF   `it is an increase of a retirement pension of a category under this Act`
          OR `it is an increase of some other pension or allowance from the Secretary of State`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): the paragraph names two kinds of pension and closes neither
    OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in the case of persons over the age of 80"

§§ `s 64 -- Attendance allowance: entitlement`

-- s 64(1A): "The following allowances are within this subsection": five are named, the list is not closed with "only".
-- src:1930 | (1A) The following allowances are within this subsection—
-- src:1931 | (a) personal independence payment;
-- src:1932 | (aa) adult disability payment;
-- src:1933 | (b) the care component of a disability living allowance.
-- src:1934 | (c) pension age disability payment.
-- src:1935 | (d) the care component of Scottish adult disability living allowance.
@ref s 64(1A) (src:1930-1935)
DECLARE `s 64(1A) Allowance` IS ONE OF
    `s 64(1A)(a) personal independence payment`
    `s 64(1A)(aa) adult disability payment`
    `s 64(1A)(b) the care component of a disability living allowance`
    `s 64(1A)(c) pension age disability payment`
    `s 64(1A)(d) the care component of Scottish adult disability living allowance`
    `s 64(1A) another allowance`    -- one that para (a) to (d) do not name

-- src:1930 | (1A) The following allowances are within this subsection—
-- src:1931 | (a) personal independence payment;
-- src:1932 | (aa) adult disability payment;
-- src:1933 | (b) the care component of a disability living allowance.
-- src:1934 | (c) pension age disability payment.
-- src:1935 | (d) the care component of Scottish adult disability living allowance.
@ref s 64(1A) (src:1930-1935)
GIVEN allowance IS A `s 64(1A) Allowance`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 64(1A) the allowance is within this subsection` MEANS
    CONSIDER allowance
    WHEN `s 64(1A)(a) personal independence payment`                                       THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 64(1A)(aa) adult disability payment`                                           THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 64(1A)(b) the care component of a disability living allowance`                 THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 64(1A)(c) pension age disability payment`                                      THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 64(1A)(d) the care component of Scottish adult disability living allowance`    THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 64(1A) another allowance` THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "The following allowances are within this subsection" has no "only"

-- s 64(2): the facts a person states about the day. "if" is the text's own condition.
-- src:1936 | (2) A person satisfies the day attendance condition if he is so severely disabled physically or mentally that, by day, he requires from another person either—
-- src:1937 | (a) frequent attention throughout the day in connection with his bodily functions, or
-- src:1938 | (b) continual supervision throughout the day in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others.
@ref s 64(2) (src:1936-1938)
DECLARE `s 64(2) Day attendance facts` HAS
    `so severely disabled physically or mentally that by day he requires attention or supervision from another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he requires frequent attention throughout the day in connection with his bodily functions`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `he requires continual supervision throughout the day in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others`   IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:1936 | (2) A person satisfies the day attendance condition if he is so severely disabled physically or mentally that, by day, he requires from another person either—
-- src:1937 | (a) frequent attention throughout the day in connection with his bodily functions, or
-- src:1938 | (b) continual supervision throughout the day in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others.
@ref s 64(2) (src:1936-1938)
GIVEN facts IS A `s 64(2) Day attendance facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 64(2) the person satisfies the day attendance condition` MEANS
    IF   facts's `so severely disabled physically or mentally that by day he requires attention or supervision from another person`
         AND (   facts's `he requires frequent attention throughout the day in connection with his bodily functions`
              OR facts's `he requires continual supervision throughout the day in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person satisfies the day attendance condition if he is so severely disabled physically or mentally that, by day, he requires from another person either"

-- src:1939 | (3) A person satisfies the night attendance condition if he is so severely disabled physically or mentally that, at night,—
-- src:1940 | (a) he requires from another person prolonged or repeated attention in connection with his bodily functions, or
-- src:1941 | (b) in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others he requires another person to be awake for a prolonged period or at frequent intervals for the purpose of watching over him.
@ref s 64(3) (src:1939-1941)
DECLARE `s 64(3) Night attendance facts` HAS
    `so severely disabled physically or mentally that at night he requires attention or watching over from another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he requires prolonged or repeated attention in connection with his bodily functions`                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `he requires another person to be awake for a prolonged period or at frequent intervals to watch over him in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:1939 | (3) A person satisfies the night attendance condition if he is so severely disabled physically or mentally that, at night,—
-- src:1940 | (a) he requires from another person prolonged or repeated attention in connection with his bodily functions, or
-- src:1941 | (b) in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others he requires another person to be awake for a prolonged period or at frequent intervals for the purpose of watching over him.
@ref s 64(3) (src:1939-1941)
GIVEN facts IS A `s 64(3) Night attendance facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 64(3) the person satisfies the night attendance condition` MEANS
    IF   facts's `so severely disabled physically or mentally that at night he requires attention or watching over from another person`
         AND (   facts's `he requires prolonged or repeated attention in connection with his bodily functions`
              OR facts's `he requires another person to be awake for a prolonged period or at frequent intervals to watch over him in order to avoid substantial danger to himself or others`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person satisfies the night attendance condition if he is so severely disabled physically or mentally that, at night"

-- s 64(1): the person has attained pensionable age on the day when that day is on or after the date they attain it (a fact about the person in the core; "pensionable age" is borrowed).
-- src:1926 | (1) A person shall be entitled to an attendance allowance if he has attained pensionable age, he is not entitled to an allowance within subsection (1A) and he satisfies either—
-- src:1927 | (a) the condition specified in subsection (2) below (“the day attendance condition”), or
-- src:1928 | (b) the condition specified in subsection (3) below (“the night attendance condition”),
-- src:1929 | and prescribed conditions as to residence and presence in Great Britain.
@ref s 64(1) (src:1926-1929)
GIVEN person IS A Person
      day IS A DATE
      `allowances the person is entitled to` IS A LIST OF `s 64(1A) Allowance`
      `day facts` IS A `s 64(2) Day attendance facts`
      `night facts` IS A `s 64(3) Night attendance facts`
      `the prescribed conditions as to residence and presence in Great Britain are satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 64(1) the person is entitled to an attendance allowance` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (day AT LEAST person's `date of attaining pensionable age`) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person shall be entitled to an attendance allowance if he has attained pensionable age"
        IF `holds an allowance within subsection (1A)` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "he is not entitled to an allowance within subsection (1A)"
        IF     (`day condition` EQUALS JUST FALSE) AND (`night condition` EQUALS JUST FALSE) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "he satisfies either"
        IF `the prescribed conditions as to residence and presence in Great Britain are satisfied` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and prescribed conditions as to residence and presence in Great Britain"
        IF     (`holds an allowance within subsection (1A)` EQUALS JUST FALSE)
           AND (   (`day condition` EQUALS JUST TRUE) OR (`night condition` EQUALS JUST TRUE))
           AND (`the prescribed conditions as to residence and presence in Great Britain are satisfied` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the prescribed conditions are made by regulations that are not among the sources; or an allowance the (1A) list does not name
    WHERE
        `day condition` MEANS `s 64(2) the person satisfies the day attendance condition` `day facts`
        `night condition` MEANS `s 64(3) the person satisfies the night attendance condition` `night facts`
        `holds an allowance within subsection (1A)` MEANS
            IF   any (`is within (1A)`) `allowances the person is entitled to`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE IF any (`is not decided by (1A)`) `allowances the person is entitled to`
                 THEN NOTHING
                 ELSE JUST FALSE
        `is within (1A)` a MEANS `s 64(1A) the allowance is within this subsection` a EQUALS JUST TRUE
        `is not decided by (1A)` a MEANS `s 64(1A) the allowance is within this subsection` a EQUALS NOTHING


-- s 64(4): "Circumstances may be prescribed in which a person is to be taken to satisfy or not to satisfy" the day and night conditions.
-- That is a regulation-making power (inert, machinery); no regulation is among the sources, so the two conditions above are worked out from the facts.

§§ `s 65 -- Attendance allowance: period and rate`

-- Calendar-month arithmetic for "6 months" (also used for the 3-month and 6-month windows of ss 66, 72 and 73).
-- The same day of the month, k months from d (k negative for earlier). Where the target month has no such day (31 August less six months
-- is "31 February") the Act does not say what the corresponding day is, so the answer is NOTHING (fork F2 in NOTES.md); `Date` would roll it forward silently.
-- src:1946 | (b) a period preceded immediately, or within such period as may be prescribed, by one of not less than 6 months throughout which he satisfied, or is likely to satisfy, one or both of those conditions.
@ref s 65(1)(b) (src:1946)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 65(1)(b) the same day of the month some months from` MEANS
    IF   DATE_DAY candidate EQUALS DATE_DAY d
    THEN JUST candidate
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Act does not say which day corresponds in a month that has no such day (fork F2)
    WHERE
        `month count` MEANS (((DATE_YEAR d) TIMES 12) PLUS (DATE_MONTH d)) MINUS 1 PLUS months
        candidate MEANS Date (DATE_DAY d) ((`month count` MODULO 12) PLUS 1) (FLOOR (`month count` DIVIDED BY 12))

-- s 65(1)(b): "a period preceded immediately ... by one of not less than 6 months": the preceding period lasts 6 months or more
-- when the day after its last day is on or after the same day six months after its first day.
-- src:1946 | (b) a period preceded immediately, or within such period as may be prescribed, by one of not less than 6 months throughout which he satisfied, or is likely to satisfy, one or both of those conditions.
@ref s 65(1)(b) (src:1946)
GIVEN period IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(1)(b) the preceding period is not less than 6 months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 65(1)(b) the same day of the month some months from` (period's `the first day`) 6
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): month-end, fork F2
    WHEN JUST `six months on` THEN
        IF   (period's `the last day` PLUS 1) AT LEAST `six months on`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "by one of not less than 6 months"

-- s 65(1): the period for which a person is entitled "shall be" a period meeting (a) and (b). It says what the period is and does not say
-- "only", so a period that does not meet them is NOTHING here (marginal reading (b)); s 64(1) already says no to a person who fails its conditions.
-- "immediately, or within such period as may be prescribed": the gap in days between the preceding period and this one is 0, or within the prescribed time (an input).
-- src:1944 | (1) Subject to the following provisions of this Act, the period for which a person is entitled to an attendance allowance shall be—
-- src:1945 | (a) a period throughout which he has satisfied or is likely to satisfy the day or the night attendance condition or both; and
-- src:1946 | (b) a period preceded immediately, or within such period as may be prescribed, by one of not less than 6 months throughout which he satisfied, or is likely to satisfy, one or both of those conditions.
@ref s 65(1) (src:1944-1946)
GIVEN `the period` IS A Period
      `throughout the period he has satisfied or is likely to satisfy the day or the night attendance condition or both` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the preceding period` IS A Period
      `throughout the preceding period he satisfied or is likely to satisfy one or both of those conditions` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the prescribed time in days within which the preceding period must have ended` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(1) the period is one for which the person is entitled to an attendance allowance` MEANS
    IF   NOT (`throughout the period he has satisfied or is likely to satisfy the day or the night attendance condition or both`)
    THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the period for which a person is entitled ... shall be" a period throughout which he has satisfied or is likely to satisfy the conditions; it does not say "only"
    ELSE IF NOT (`throughout the preceding period he satisfied or is likely to satisfy one or both of those conditions`)
         THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): para (b) "shall be" a period preceded by one throughout which he satisfied the conditions
         ELSE CONSIDER `s 65(1)(b) the preceding period is not less than 6 months` `the preceding period`
              WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): month-end, fork F2
              WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "preceded immediately ... by one of not less than 6 months"
              WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
                  IF   `gap in days` EQUALS 0
                  THEN JUST TRUE
                  ELSE IF `gap in days` LESS THAN 0
                       THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the preceding period overlaps this one, which the text does not address
                       ELSE CONSIDER `the prescribed time in days within which the preceding period must have ended`
                            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "within such period as may be prescribed"
                            WHEN JUST limit THEN
                                IF   `gap in days` AT MOST limit
                                THEN JUST TRUE
                                ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "within such period as may be prescribed"
    WHERE
        `gap in days` MEANS ((Day (`the period`'s `the first day`)) MINUS (Day (`the preceding period`'s `the last day`))) MINUS 1

-- s 65(2): a person with renal failure undergoing prescribed treatment "shall, in such circumstances as may be prescribed, be deemed" to satisfy the conditions.
-- src:1947 | (2) For the purposes of subsection (1) above a person who suffers from renal failure and is undergoing such form of treatment as may be prescribed shall, in such circumstances as may be prescribed, be deemed to satisfy or to be likely to satisfy the day or the night attendance condition or both.
@ref s 65(2) (src:1947)
GIVEN `the person suffers from renal failure` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is undergoing a form of treatment that is prescribed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the circumstances are those prescribed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(2) the person is deemed to satisfy or to be likely to satisfy the day or the night attendance condition or both` MEANS
    IF   `the person suffers from renal failure`
         AND (`the person is undergoing a form of treatment that is prescribed` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
         AND (`the circumstances are those prescribed` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the treatment and the circumstances are prescribed by regulations that are not among the sources; the rule only deems and does not say a person outside it fails

-- s 65(3): "shall be the higher rate ... if both as regards that period and as regards the period of 6 months ... he has satisfied or is likely to satisfy both the day and the night attendance conditions, and shall be the lower rate in any other case."
-- The text itself names the lower rate for every other case. The two rates are in Schedule 4, whose table is omitted from this text, so each is an input (S-new in NOTES.md).
-- src:1948 | (3) The weekly rate of the attendance allowance payable to a person for any period shall be the higher rate specified in Schedule 4, Part III, paragraph 1, if both as regards that period and as regards the period of 6 months mentioned in subsection (1)(b) above he has satisfied or is likely to satisfy both the day and the night attendance conditions, and shall be the lower rate in any other case.
@ref s 65(3) (src:1948)
GIVEN `both conditions are satisfied or likely to be satisfied for the period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `both conditions are satisfied or likely to be satisfied for the 6 months mentioned in subsection (1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `sch 4 pt III para 1 the higher rate of attendance allowance in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `sch 4 pt III para 1 the lower rate of attendance allowance in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 65(3) the weekly rate of the attendance allowance in pounds` MEANS
    IF   `both conditions are satisfied or likely to be satisfied for the period`
         AND `both conditions are satisfied or likely to be satisfied for the 6 months mentioned in subsection (1)(b)`
    THEN `sch 4 pt III para 1 the higher rate of attendance allowance in pounds`    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text) if the Schedule 4 table figure is not supplied
    ELSE `sch 4 pt III para 1 the lower rate of attendance allowance in pounds`    -- the text: "shall be the lower rate in any other case"

-- s 65(4): a flat negative limited by "for any period preceding the date on which he makes or is treated as making a claim".
-- BAR POLARITY: the function answers "is the person barred?": JUST TRUE = the text says not entitled; JUST FALSE = the text says no bar;
-- NOTHING = the rule is silent on the case.
-- src:1949 | (4) A person shall not be entitled to an attendance allowance for any period preceding the date on which he makes or is treated as making a claim for it.
@ref s 65(4) (src:1949)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `the date on which he makes or is treated as making a claim` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(4) the person is barred from an attendance allowance for the day by the date of claim` MEANS
    IF   day LESS THAN `the date on which he makes or is treated as making a claim`
    THEN JUST TRUE    -- the text says no in its own words (a flat negative): "A person shall not be entitled to an attendance allowance for any period preceding the date on which he makes or is treated as making a claim for it"
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar covers "any period preceding the date" of the claim and says nothing of a later day

-- s 65(5): regulations may provide for entitlement for a period before the claim (a regulation-making power; inert, machinery).

-- s 65(6)(a): "a claim for an attendance allowance may be made during the period of 6 months immediately preceding the period for which the person ... is entitled".
-- A permission limited by its own wording: a claim outside the window is NOTHING. The window is the 6 months ending the day before the period begins.
-- src:1951 | (6) Except in so far as regulations otherwise provide and subject to section 66(1) below—
-- src:1952 | (a) a claim for an attendance allowance may be made during the period of 6 months immediately preceding the period for which the person to whom the claim relates is entitled to the allowance; and
@ref s 65(6)(a) (src:1951-1952)
GIVEN `the first day of the period for which the person is entitled` IS A DATE
      `the day the claim is made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(6)(a) the claim may be made on the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 65(1)(b) the same day of the month some months from` `the first day of the period for which the person is entitled` (0 MINUS 6)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): month-end, fork F2
    WHEN JUST `window opens` THEN
        IF   (`the day the claim is made` AT LEAST `window opens`)
             AND (`the day the claim is made` LESS THAN `the first day of the period for which the person is entitled`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may be made during the period of 6 months immediately preceding"; "Except in so far as regulations otherwise provide"

-- s 65(6)(a) as a regulative rule: the person to whom the claim relates MAY make the claim within the window (time counted in days from the day the window opens, day 0).
-- The window ends the day before the period begins.
-- src:1951 | (6) Except in so far as regulations otherwise provide and subject to section 66(1) below—
-- src:1952 | (a) a claim for an attendance allowance may be made during the period of 6 months immediately preceding the period for which the person to whom the claim relates is entitled to the allowance; and
@ref s 65(6)(a) (src:1951-1952)
DECLARE `s 65(6)(a) Party` IS ONE OF
    `s 65(6)(a) the person to whom the claim relates`

-- src:1951 | (6) Except in so far as regulations otherwise provide and subject to section 66(1) below—
-- src:1952 | (a) a claim for an attendance allowance may be made during the period of 6 months immediately preceding the period for which the person to whom the claim relates is entitled to the allowance; and
@ref s 65(6)(a) (src:1951-1952)
DECLARE `s 65(6)(a) Act` IS ONE OF
    `s 65(6)(a) makes the claim for an attendance allowance`

-- src:1951 | (6) Except in so far as regulations otherwise provide and subject to section 66(1) below—
-- src:1952 | (a) a claim for an attendance allowance may be made during the period of 6 months immediately preceding the period for which the person to whom the claim relates is entitled to the allowance; and
@ref s 65(6)(a) (src:1951-1952)
GIVEN `the day the window opens` IS A DATE
      `the first day of the period for which the person is entitled` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `s 65(6)(a) Party` `s 65(6)(a) Act`
`s 65(6)(a) the person may claim in advance` MEANS
    PARTY `s 65(6)(a) the person to whom the claim relates`
    MAY `s 65(6)(a) makes the claim for an attendance allowance`
    WITHIN (((Day `the first day of the period for which the person is entitled`) MINUS (Day `the day the window opens`)) MINUS 1)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

-- s 65(6)(b): the award on such a claim is "subject to the condition that, throughout that period of 6 months, that person satisfies" (i) both conditions or (ii) if the award is at the lower rate, one.
-- src:1953 | (b) an award may be made in pursuance of a claim so made, subject to the condition that, throughout that period of 6 months, that person satisfies—
-- src:1954 | (i) both the day and the night attendance conditions, or
-- src:1955 | (ii) if the award is at the lower rate, one of those conditions.
@ref s 65(6)(b) (src:1953-1955)
GIVEN `throughout the 6 months he satisfies both the day and the night attendance conditions` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the award is at the lower rate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `throughout the 6 months he satisfies one of those conditions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(6)(b) the condition on an award made on a claim made in advance is satisfied` MEANS
    IF   `throughout the 6 months he satisfies both the day and the night attendance conditions`
         OR (`the award is at the lower rate` AND `throughout the 6 months he satisfies one of those conditions`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "subject to the condition that, throughout that period of 6 months, that person satisfies"

-- s 65(7): a person to whom either Regulation applies "shall not be entitled ... for a period unless during that period the United Kingdom is competent".
-- BAR POLARITY as above.
-- src:1956 | (7) A person to whom either Regulation (EC) No 1408/71 or Regulation /(EC) No 883/2004 applies shall not be entitled to an attendance allowance for a period unless during that period the United Kingdom is competent for payment of sickness benefits in cash to the person for the purposes of Chapter 1 of Title III of the Regulation in question.
@ref s 65(7) (src:1956)
GIVEN `Regulation (EC) No 1408/71 or Regulation (EC) No 883/2004 applies to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the United Kingdom is competent for payment of sickness benefits in cash to the person during the period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 65(7) the person is barred from an attendance allowance for the period by the Regulations` MEANS
    IF   NOT (`Regulation (EC) No 1408/71 or Regulation (EC) No 883/2004 applies to the person`)
    THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar applies only to "A person to whom either Regulation ... applies"
    ELSE IF `the United Kingdom is competent for payment of sickness benefits in cash to the person during the period`
         THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not be entitled to an attendance allowance for a period unless during that period the United Kingdom is competent for payment of sickness benefits in cash"
         ELSE JUST TRUE    -- the text says no (a flat negative with "unless" not met): "shall not be entitled to an attendance allowance for a period unless"

§§ `s 66 -- Attendance allowance for the terminally ill`

-- s 66(2)(a): "terminally ill" at a time if at that time he suffers from a progressive disease and his death in consequence can reasonably be expected within 12 months.
-- The expectation of death is a medical judgment the Act does not hand to anyone by name; it is a plain input.
-- src:1964 | (a) a person is “terminally ill” at any time if at that time he suffers from a progressive disease and his death in consequence of that disease can reasonably be expected within 12 months; and
@ref s 66(2)(a) (src:1964)
GIVEN `at that time he suffers from a progressive disease` IS A BOOLEAN
      `his death in consequence of that disease can reasonably be expected within 12 months` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 66(2)(a) the person is terminally ill at the time` MEANS
    IF   `at that time he suffers from a progressive disease`
         AND `his death in consequence of that disease can reasonably be expected within 12 months`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person is “terminally ill” at any time if at that time he suffers from a progressive disease and his death in consequence of that disease can reasonably be expected within 12 months"

-- s 66(1)(a)(i): on a claim made expressly on the ground of terminal illness, he "shall be taken" to satisfy both conditions for so much of the period of terminal illness as does not fall before the date of the claim.
-- src:1958 | (1) If a terminally ill person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is such a person, then—
-- src:1959 | (a) he shall be taken—
-- src:1960 | (i) to satisfy, or to be likely to satisfy, both the day attendance condition and the night attendance condition for so much of the period for which he is terminally ill as does not fall before the date of the claim; and
@ref s 66(1)(a)(i) (src:1958-1960)
GIVEN `the person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is terminally ill` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is terminally ill on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      day IS A DATE
      `the date of the claim` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 66(1)(a)(i) the person is taken to satisfy both the day attendance condition and the night attendance condition on the day` MEANS
    IF   `the person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is terminally ill`
         AND `the person is terminally ill on the day`
         AND (day AT LEAST `the date of the claim`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "so much of the period for which he is terminally ill as does not fall before the date of the claim"; the rule deems and does not say a person outside it fails

-- s 66(1)(a)(ii): taken to have satisfied the conditions "for the period of 6 months immediately preceding the date of the claim or, if later, the first date on which he is terminally ill".
-- src:1958 | (1) If a terminally ill person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is such a person, then—
-- src:1961 | (ii) to have satisfied those conditions for the period of 6 months immediately preceding the date of the claim or, if later, the first date on which he is terminally ill (so however that no allowance shall be payable by virtue of this sub-paragraph for any period preceding that date); and
@ref s 66(1)(a)(ii) (src:1958-1961)
GIVEN `the date of the claim` IS A DATE
      `the first date on which he is terminally ill` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 66(1)(a)(ii) the first day of the period for which the person is taken to have satisfied the conditions` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 65(1)(b) the same day of the month some months from` `the date of the claim` (0 MINUS 6)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): month-end, fork F2
    WHEN JUST `six months before` THEN
        IF   `the first date on which he is terminally ill` AT LEAST `six months before`
        THEN JUST `the first date on which he is terminally ill`
        ELSE JUST `six months before`

-- s 66(1)(b): "the period for which he is entitled to attendance allowance shall be so much of the period for which he is terminally ill as does not fall before the date of the claim."
-- src:1958 | (1) If a terminally ill person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is such a person, then—
-- src:1962 | (b) the period for which he is entitled to attendance allowance shall be so much of the period for which he is terminally ill as does not fall before the date of the claim.
@ref s 66(1)(b) (src:1958-1962)
GIVEN `the person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is terminally ill` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is terminally ill on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      day IS A DATE
      `the date of the claim` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 66(1)(b) the day is in the period for which the person is entitled to attendance allowance` MEANS
    IF   `the person makes a claim expressly on the ground that he is terminally ill`
         AND `the person is terminally ill on the day`
         AND (day AT LEAST `the date of the claim`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "shall be so much of the period for which he is terminally ill as does not fall before the date of the claim"; it states the period and does not say "only"

-- s 66(2)(b): where a person purports to make the claim on behalf of another, "that other shall be regarded as making the claim, notwithstanding that it is made without his knowledge or authority".
-- src:1965 | (b) where a person purports to make a claim for an attendance allowance by virtue of that subsection on behalf of another, that other shall be regarded as making the claim, notwithstanding that it is made without his knowledge or authority.
@ref s 66(2)(b) (src:1965)
GIVEN `a person purports to make a claim for an attendance allowance by virtue of subsection (1) on behalf of another` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 66(2)(b) the other person is regarded as making the claim` MEANS
    IF   `a person purports to make a claim for an attendance allowance by virtue of subsection (1) on behalf of another`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the deeming applies "where a person purports to make a claim ... on behalf of another"

§§ `s 67 -- Exclusions by regulation`

-- s 67(1), (2): regulations may provide that a person undergoing renal-failure treatment is deemed not to satisfy the conditions, and that attendance allowance is not payable
-- for a period when he is a resident of a care home at public cost. Those are regulation-making powers (inert, machinery). Their defined terms are encoded below.

-- s 67(3): "The reference in subsection (2) to a care home is to an establishment that provides accommodation together with nursing or personal care."
-- src:1969 | (3) The reference in subsection (2) to a care home is to an establishment that provides accommodation together with nursing or personal care.
@ref s 67(3) (src:1969)
GIVEN `the establishment provides accommodation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the establishment provides nursing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the establishment provides personal care` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 67(3) the establishment is a care home` MEANS
    IF   `the establishment provides accommodation`
         AND (`the establishment provides nursing` OR `the establishment provides personal care`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The reference in subsection (2) to a care home is to an establishment that provides accommodation together with nursing or personal care"

-- s 67(4): "The following are qualifying services": three are named, the list is not closed with "only".
-- src:1970 | (4) The following are qualifying services for the purposes of subsection (2)—
-- src:1971 | (a) accommodation,
-- src:1972 | (b) board, and
-- src:1973 | (c) personal care.
@ref s 67(4) (src:1970-1973)
DECLARE `s 67(4) Service` IS ONE OF
    `s 67(4)(a) accommodation`
    `s 67(4)(b) board`
    `s 67(4)(c) personal care`
    `s 67(4) another service`

-- src:1970 | (4) The following are qualifying services for the purposes of subsection (2)—
-- src:1971 | (a) accommodation,
-- src:1972 | (b) board, and
-- src:1973 | (c) personal care.
@ref s 67(4) (src:1970-1973)
GIVEN service IS A `s 67(4) Service`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 67(4) the service is a qualifying service for the purposes of subsection (2)` MEANS
    CONSIDER service
    WHEN `s 67(4)(a) accommodation` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 67(4)(b) board` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 67(4)(c) personal care` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 67(4) another service` THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "The following are qualifying services" has no "only"

-- s 67(5): "The reference in subsection (2) to a specified enactment is to an enactment which is, or is of a description, specified for the purposes of that subsection by regulations."
-- src:1974 | (5) The reference in subsection (2) to a specified enactment is to an enactment which is, or is of a description, specified for the purposes of that subsection by regulations.
@ref s 67(5) (src:1974)
GIVEN `the enactment is, or is of a description, specified for the purposes of subsection (2) by regulations` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 67(5) the enactment is a specified enactment` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the enactment is, or is of a description, specified for the purposes of subsection (2) by regulations`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the regulations that specify enactments are not among the sources
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is to an enactment which is, or is of a description, specified"

-- s 67(6): the power to specify an enactment includes power to specify it for a particular purpose only (a regulation-making power; inert, machinery).

-- s 67(7): "In this section, “enactment” includes an enactment comprised in, or in an instrument made under, an Act of the Scottish Parliament." "includes" does not close the term.
-- src:1976 | (7) In this section, “enactment” includes an enactment comprised in, or in an instrument made under, an Act of the Scottish Parliament.
@ref s 67(7) (src:1976)
GIVEN `the enactment is comprised in, or in an instrument made under, an Act of the Scottish Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 67(7) the provision is within the word enactment in this section` MEANS
    IF   `the enactment is comprised in, or in an instrument made under, an Act of the Scottish Parliament`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "“enactment” includes"; other enactments are not excluded by this sentence
